  "description": "Train base estimators, assemble out-of-fold predictions into meta-features, and generate final predictions using a meta-classifier.\n\nStacking blends heterogeneous model families (trees, linear, KNN) by training a meta-model on their collective predictions.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\hat{y} = g\\left(f_1(x), f_2(x), \\dots, f_M(x)\\right)\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Generate predictions from multiple diverse base models\n2. Stack base predictions into a second-level meta-feature matrix\n3. Train meta-learner over assembled features to predict target classes\n\n### Returns\nList of integer ensemble class predictions.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Stacking Ensemble\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nIn computational mathematics and machine learning, robust algorithmic routines ensure calculations are both mathematically sound and computationally efficient. **Stacking Ensemble** implements a core computational building block.\n\nStacking blends heterogeneous model families (trees, linear, KNN) by training a meta-model on their collective predictions. In this problem, the goal is to: train base estimators, assemble out-of-fold predictions into meta-features, and generate final predictions using a meta-classifier.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\hat{y} = g\\left(f_1(x), f_2(x), \\dots, f_M(x)\\right)\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Generate predictions from multiple diverse base models\n2. **Phase 2**: Stack base predictions into a second-level meta-feature matrix\n3. **Phase 3**: Train meta-learner over assembled features to predict target classes\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Algorithmic Complexity**: Designing routines with optimal time and space complexity ensures they scale gracefully to large datasets.\n* **Numerical Accuracy**: Safeguarding calculations against floating-point underflow or division by zero ensures consistent results.\n* **Vectorization**: Implementing operations with contiguous array buffers avoids interpreter overhead.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: X_train = [[1.0, 2.0], [2.0, 1.0], [5.0, 6.0], [6.0, 5.0]], y_train = [0, 0, 1, 1], X_test = [[1.5, 1.5], [5.5, 5.5]]\n\nOutput: [1, 1]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated ml_stacking under standard test conditions."
